  "live_records": {
    "q-90392": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90392",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}},\\,x>1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2},$ compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{3}{x})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(3-\\frac{3}{a}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1 \\\\  & =3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =3+3-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
