      "problem": "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{4}(1-x),\\,-2\\le x\\le 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$",
      "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-[4+\\frac{32}{5}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{52}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
